WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.380
- I think that from a very young age,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.800
watching anime and seeing
those depictions of friendship,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.320
like I didn't have like a
lot of friends growing up

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.420
and I don't have a huge
amount of friends now,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.140
but the intensity with
which I love my friends,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.490
the amount of time, and effort,

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.200
and energy I put into my friendships,

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.544
I think has been like
directly fed to me from anime.

00:00:22.544 --> 00:00:23.377
I feel like anime taught me

00:00:23.377 --> 00:00:24.360
you don't need to have a lot of friends,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.180
you just need to have the
people that understand you,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.040
that love you at both your best and worst,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.150
that would take a bullet for you

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.400
and that you would take a bullet for.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.710
And that's how I feel about my friends.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.120
Like we cannot talk for three months,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.170
and if they called me and they're like,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.000
hey look, I got a body I need to bury,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.173
I'd be like, bet, say less.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:41.006
(bright music)

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:43.650
- You are listening to the
"MANA Podcast" from Netflix.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.390
I'm your host, Matt Owens.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.160
I'm a screenwriter, co-show runner

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.560
on the upcoming live action
adaptation of "One Piece"

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.430
from Netflix, and most
importantly, a lifelong anime fan.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.810
The "MANA Podcast" stands for

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.360
Manga, Anime, Netflix Adaptation,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.280
and it's an opportunity
for us to talk about

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.710
our love of all things manga and anime.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.840
I've invited some of my
closest friends to chat.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.580
We're gonna keep this fun and casual,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.250
and we're really glad that
you've decided to join us.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.770
This is the "MANA Podcast."

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.230
Oh, and also there will be spoilers,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.565
so check the podcast
description for that list.

00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:18.732
(bright upbeat music)

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.560
Joining me on the MANA Podcast today,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.630
you know her, you love her,

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.220
from "Reign" to "Grey's Anatomy,"
my friend, Adelaide Kane.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.665
- Hi, buddy, how you doin'?

00:01:31.665 --> 00:01:33.270
- I'm doing well.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.030
Why don't you introduce
yourself a little bit

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.650
to our audience.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.760
- I'm Adelaide Kane.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.917
You might know me from such
projects as "Teen Wolf",

00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:45.240
"Reign", "Grey's Anatomy",
"Once Upon a Time,"

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.770
and you know that one movie

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.630
where people killed each other a bunch?

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.730
There's several of those movies actually,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.320
had a long history in B-grade horror

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.180
before my career took off.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.560
I've been a long-time actor
and a longer time anime fan

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.600
since I was like a kid.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.970
I remember in like my early tween years

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.100
sneaking into the living
room with my little brother

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.200
to turn on "Adult Swim" on Cartoon Network

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.600
to watch like "Trigun" and "Cowboy Bebop."

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.000
'Cause that's what Adult Swim
was back when I was a teenager

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.487
in like the early 2000s.

00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:16.757
It was the only way that we
could watch anime back then.

00:02:16.757 --> 00:02:18.930
And in my hometown a volume of manga

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.390
was like $20 'cause it was all imported.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.610
So I got a job very
young so I could afford

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.960
to buy volumes of manga

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.310
that I would read in 15 minutes,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.860
and then I'd have to wait
a month for the next volume

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.150
to come into the one store
in Perth that sold Manga.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.364
So those are my credentials,

00:02:34.364 --> 00:02:37.260
and yeah, that's why I'm here, you know,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.140
chop it up about some
of my favorite shows.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.360
- So what would you say your favorite is?

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.240
What is the one anime or manga for you?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.320
- Hmm, it's really
difficult to just pick one,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.170
because I have like the old
school anime that I started on

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.210
that always has a special
place in my heart,

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.500
and then like the newest stuff,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.480
there's some really cool like new stuff

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.597
that's been coming out lately like,

00:02:58.597 --> 00:03:00.270
"So I'm a Spider, So What?"

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.340
I really loved "Kotaro Lives Alone",

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.410
like "Spy x Family" I'm obsessed with.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:05.922
I really love "Hunter X Hunter,"

00:03:05.922 --> 00:03:07.470
and I know it's like huh,
everybody loves "Hunter X Hunter."

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.850
Well I do, okay?

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.827
I do, I don't wanna hear
about it from anime purists.

00:03:11.827 --> 00:03:13.680
- "Yu Yu Hakusho's" better.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.230
- Oh my god, are we gonna
get into a debate about this

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.606
on the podcast?
- It's the hill

00:03:17.606 --> 00:03:18.439
that I'm gonna die on.

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:20.880
- Okay, well you will die alone. (laughs)

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.860
So I don't know, I
couldn't pick a favorite.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.500
I think the one that really
stuck with me when I was a kid,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.330
so much so that I named my cat

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.030
after the title character is "Ranma 1/2."

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.199
And that's like a old-school
sort of episodic style,

00:03:34.199 --> 00:03:38.790
gender bending martial arts
anime from like the '90s.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.280
And not a lot of people
understand that reference.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.237
I'm like, "Yeah, my cat's name is Ranma,

00:03:42.237 --> 00:03:44.550
and they're like, "Ramna, Ramdan?"

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.080
And I'm like, "Ramna."

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.600
And not a lot of people get
that that's what I've named him

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.370
after that was sort of like, you know,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.410
the beginning of the end for me.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.750
And I've been watching
anime ever since. Yeah.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.477
So if I had to pick a favorite,
I guess I'd pick that one.

00:03:56.477 --> 00:03:57.420
- That's a great one.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.900
I remember going to Barnes
and Noble back in the day

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.570
and just, you would only look
at whatever was available

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.250
and it was a really limited selection,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.020
but Ramna was one of them.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.630
It was like right up there
right next to like "Inuyasha"

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.730
and a bunch of early classic-
- Yeah,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.490
it's like in that pocket,
like during that time.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.560
And I remember very
distinctly it was, you know,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.540
before they figured out
that they needed to put

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.700
age restrictions on manga,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.790
it was one of the few manga

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.930
that like had boobs in it. (laughs)

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:22.763
- Yep.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.710
- And now they have age
restrictions on stuff.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.770
But oh my god, walking
into Barnes and Noble

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.040
into the manga section, it was wild.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.140
It was wild out there up until sort of,

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.210
I think like 2010 when they figured out

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.870
there was some really adult
content in some of that manga.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.960
Like, I read stuff like
online and in manga

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.820
that I was much too young to be reading

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.150
'cause they did not
cotton on to the content

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.460
until I guess it became more popular.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.480
And it's just so nuts because
now anime and manga is cool,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.210
and popular, and it's a direct correlation

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.680
between the K-pop stands

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.170
and K-pop becoming such a huge thing.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.717
And then like sort of by
default manga and anime,

00:05:03.717 --> 00:05:04.950
and that kind of culture

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.470
has now also become cool and mainstream.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:08.310
And I'm like, when was this?

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.580
When I was in high school
I was mocked relentlessly

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.110
for liking anime and manga.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.610
People thought I was a little creep.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.010
And to be fair I was. (laughs)

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.470
But you know, it's so funny

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.930
'cause that stigma of being an anime fan

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.280
from when I was in high school, it's like,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.103
it's cool now and I'm like,
where was this when I,

00:05:26.103 --> 00:05:27.563
I could have been cool,
I could've been cool.

00:05:29.246 --> 00:05:30.270
And it's really great.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.060
It's so fun just like
watching all these kids

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.270
wander around in their
"One Piece" t-shirts

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.120
and they're like, you know
"Dragon Ball" t-shirts and stuff,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.350
and I'm like, you don't even know,

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.270
you don't even know what
the struggle was like.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.740
- What we had to go through.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.680
- What we had to go through! (groans)

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.513
- But that's also, I mean,

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.310
I think that's how growing
up being interested

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.100
in this stuff, that's
how we found our friends.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.780
That's how we found our groups.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.280
Who are the other people
that are being bullied

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.800
for wearing Hot Topic
"Dragon Ball" t-shirts and,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.487
- Yeah.
- going home early to watch

00:06:00.487 --> 00:06:01.830
"Sailor Moon" episodes.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.710
- Yeah, I mean Australia's
like a very athletic culture

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.220
and country, and even just
being really into reading books.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.380
So like my little nerd
group in high school,

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.320
we all had like very
specific special interests.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.640
I didn't really have a lot of friends

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.650
that was super into
sort of anime and manga,

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.020
the friends that I did have
were like in the boarding school

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.880
and they were all from like
Malaysia and Indonesia,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.130
and they would give me
these computer CD ROMs

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.620
full of manga.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.990
And I got banned from using the computer

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.460
because I wouldn't do any of my work,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.320
I would just like read
volumes, and volumes,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.980
and volumes, and volumes of manga

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.310
in like these massive like
four inch tall CD sleeves

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.540
of manga disks that my
friends would give me.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.550
It's so funny now,
especially with the internet,

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.260
it's like even if you're in a small town

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.730
where nobody likes the things you like,

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.040
you can have a whole community online.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.650
And I think with the
popularization of anime,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.150
and like, animation in general,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.800
especially like adult animation,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.990
combined with the
availability of streaming,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.850
the community's just much, much bigger

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:57.930
and there's a lot more available.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.330
Like you don't have to
go on sketchy websites

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.785
like "Manga Cat" to read your manga.

00:07:02.785 --> 00:07:04.230
- Manga Cat.
- There's now whole dedicated

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.700
sections in the bookstore,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.710
and it's really easily accessible online,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.590
and you can sign up for
"Mad Comics," whatever,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.960
or "Shonen Jump" and read in real time

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:13.793
as things come out.

00:07:13.793 --> 00:07:16.170
You don't have to wait months,
and months, and months.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:19.080
It's awesome. There's just
so much out there as well.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.690
And we only, like here in
the English speaking world,

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.600
on like Netflix or other
streaming platforms,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.090
we only have a microcosm of
the content that's available

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.250
that has been either dubbed into English

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:30.870
or subbed into English.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.840
So it's really fantastic.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.480
Like you could just spend all
day at home watching anime

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:37.470
and not having a life,

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.220
which I definitely don't
do at all. (laughs)

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.160
- And you're right, the
creation of those friendships,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.210
those communities, that
is one of the best things

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.040
about manga and anime.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.710
And that kind of brings us to the theme

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.870
that I wanna talk to
you about today, Addie.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:56.571
- Nakama, the power of friendship.

00:07:56.571 --> 00:07:58.320
- The power of friendship.
(Adelaide laughs)

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.620
It's something that is very important.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.820
That term is really closely
tied to "One Piece,"

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:10.320
and it's used in a way, it
describes friendship and family,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.900
but at a level that
goes beyond those terms.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.370
It's the people that it doesn't matter

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.560
if you are related to them or not,

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.270
but it's your ride or die.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.600
It's the people that you
would do anything for.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.530
And the concept is something
that really permeates

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.660
a lot of series.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:28.493
You know, people we do,

00:08:28.493 --> 00:08:30.840
we joke about the power
of friendship, but it's-

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.640
- Yeah, 'cause it's cheesy, but it's true.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.690
- It is, it's true.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:38.430
And from "One Piece" to "Seven
Deadly Sins" to "Fairy Tail,'

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:41.370
it's like that bond,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:46.370
that connection truly is
one of the greatest powers

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.070
and the greatest emotions that exist.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:53.727
So like what is, when you
talk about the idea of nakama,

00:08:53.727 --> 00:08:55.110
and like the power of friendship,

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.220
what does that mean to you?

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.960
- I think that from a very young age,

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.150
watching anime and seeing, you know,

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.410
those depictions of friendship,

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.930
like I didn't have a lot
of friends growing up,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.000
and I don't have a huge
amount of friends now,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.387
but the intensity with
which I love my friends,

00:09:09.387 --> 00:09:11.610
and the amount of time,
and effort, and energy

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:12.810
I put into my friendships,

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.407
I think has been like
directly fed to me from anime.

00:09:15.407 --> 00:09:16.680
I feel like anime taught me

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.940
you don't need to have a lot of friends,

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.790
you just need to have the
people that understand you,

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.650
that love you at both your best and worst,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:23.730
that would take a bullet for you,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.010
and that you would take a bullet for.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.320
And that's how I feel about my friends.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.700
Like we cannot talk for three months,

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.590
and if they called me and
they're like, hey look,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:34.470
I got a body I need to bury,
I'd be like, bet, say less.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.000
And we show up with a shovel, you know?

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.760
I think that it's also like
a really important lesson,

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.830
especially as you grow up, you know,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.160
as all of us grow up and
we leave home and you know,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:49.160
we start our own lives, your
friends do become your family.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.050
They become your connection
to the outside world.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.260
They become your connection to
your heart and your emotion.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.110
I would say even more so
than romantic relationships.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.270
Like I invest a lot of time
and effort in my friends

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.420
because in my mind they're
gonna be there forever.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.610
You know, like those
are gonna be my people

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.800
for the rest of my life.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.890
And I think it's really cool
that anime sort of sets you up

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:12.360
for that in a weird way.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.010
Your friendships, like
finding the right people,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.430
finding people who understand
you and who you understand

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:19.500
is so important in adulthood.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.900
And as you go through life, you know,

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.730
'cause you leave your parents'
house and your siblings

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:25.140
and you all start your own lives,

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.770
and just maintaining that
connection with other human beings

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.140
and finding people who might
just sort of understand you

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.880
better or teach you new
things about yourself,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:33.750
or about the world,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.030
is like a really integral
part of growing up.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:37.560
Whether you do that in real life,

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.177
whether you have that in
your online community,

00:10:39.177 --> 00:10:42.090
and the amount of support
that you can find online

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:43.830
and through special interests

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.710
is really not to be
discounted in a world like,

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:49.860
especially post-pandemic
where all of our social skills

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.270
have gotten extraordinarily rusty,

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.610
those of us who had any to begin with.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.610
And since we're anime stans,
that's really up for debate.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.520
But yeah, it's a fantastic
thing to sort of instill

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.970
in kids and young people is
that friendship is important.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:03.840
And not only that,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.660
but friendship through good times and bad.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.670
I think in a world where
we can feel more and more

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.120
disconnected from our
fellow man and the world,

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.177
it's a really beautiful part
of what makes anime anime.

00:11:15.177 --> 00:11:18.570
And I think for a lot of us,
I can only speak for myself,

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.850
but for a lot of us that
were like anime fans

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.010
at a time when anime wasn't cool,

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.770
that idea or that fantasy of friendship

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.170
was so important to me.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.210
Like that was something
I saw and I wanted,

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:31.980
I wanted it so badly.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:36.980
And I worked really hard to
find people to have as family,

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:41.670
especially moving across
the planet when I was 19,

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:45.150
quite literally the other side
of the planet to Los Angeles,

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.760
I was like, I need friends.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.555
I want that. I want those people.

00:11:49.555 --> 00:11:52.500
I'm across the world from
everyone and everything

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.740
I've ever known, and the
only way I'll feel at home

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:00.360
in this strange land that is
the United States of America

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.190
is if I find my people.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.390
- One of the things that you brought up

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.180
that I think is really
interesting is, you know,

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.940
a lot of main characters in anime series,

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.510
especially in the Shonen
genre, suffer from, you know,

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:13.800
dead parent syndrome.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:14.633
They don't really have

00:12:14.633 --> 00:12:16.560
families to speak of.
- Ugh, god no!

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.542
So it's that found family

00:12:18.542 --> 00:12:20.550
- Exactly.
- trope is so important.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.500
And you know, I think that that is like,

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.620
anime does hammer home the
importance of community,

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.480
which I think some of us forget about,

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.510
like how important that
is to have community.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.940
I hate the dead parents
thing 'cause I'm like,

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:33.900
couldn't you just,

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:38.070
or it's like a single parent
or like an abusive parent.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.812
Like even, in everything,
there's always dead parents.

00:12:40.812 --> 00:12:44.640
- There's always some, at
least one person who is dead.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.090
But that it goes to show that
like you can have these really

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:52.890
unconventional or sad
familial lives or upbringings,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.160
but finding your people is possible

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.780
and that connection can be-

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.040
- Yeah, there's someone out there,

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.215
there's someone out there who
will be that family for you.

00:13:02.215 --> 00:13:03.048
And what that means-
- And what's also nice,

00:13:03.048 --> 00:13:05.580
it shows you, it's not always perfect

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.560
'cause no family is, no friendship is,

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:08.393
no relationship is.

00:13:08.393 --> 00:13:10.320
'Cause you look at people
like Naruto and Sasuke,

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.250
who are best friends

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.140
but have literally
tried to kill each other

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.330
at multiple times throughout their lives.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:16.500
- Oh yeah, no, totally.

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.360
- Or look at Zorro and Sanji,

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:19.620
dudes hate each other.
- Oh my god,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.963
beat the shit out of each
each other on a regular basis.

00:13:21.963 --> 00:13:23.790
- But they have each other's backs.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:24.623
- Exactly.

00:13:24.623 --> 00:13:27.450
- There's a wonderful
moment in the Wano arc

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.510
of the manga recently where
Sanji's really dealing with

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.250
the fact that he might
be becoming the monster

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:34.020
that his father tried to turn him into.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:38.730
And he tells Zoro, "Hey man,
if things go sideways for me,

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:40.830
I need you to kill me."

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.530
And that just shows such a
trust between the two of them.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.380
These guys were always
at each other's throats.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.090
- It's like a profound act of trust.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:49.620
- Yeah.
- And vulnerability.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.740
- And it takes a real friend
to be able to do something.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:54.120
Like, you're closest friends
- To be able to even have

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.250
- are the ones that are like-
- that conversation,

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.050
and like ask that, and not only that,

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:00.330
but like the level of
dedication and connection

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:03.810
to respond to that request
with, I've got you.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.050
Because like, even as painful
as that would be for me,

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:09.420
it would be more painful for me to,

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:14.010
to see you become the thing
that you fear the most.

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:14.843
- And you know,

00:14:14.843 --> 00:14:16.800
that's one of the great things
about our real relationships

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.840
and friendships is a
good friend is someone

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.120
who will support you and
prop you up and things,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.140
but is also someone that's gonna be like,

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:23.940
hey man, cut it out.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.940
- Go to therapy, figure
it out, stop it, stop!

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.800
I've had my friends do
that to me and I apologize,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.067
and I'm like, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:14:33.067 --> 00:14:34.248
- You're right, you're right.
- You right, you're right,

00:14:34.248 --> 00:14:35.352
you're right, you're right.

00:14:35.352 --> 00:14:38.070
- Alright, so Naruto and
Sasuke, Zoro and Sanji,

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.390
those are some pretty good examples.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.510
What are some other examples
of this idea of nakama

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.100
that like really resonate with you?

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:48.360
- Hmm, you know what really
just punched me so aggressively

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.327
in the feels?

00:14:49.327 --> 00:14:51.750
"Kotaro Lives Alone."

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.060
I'm not a watering pot,
I like don't cry ever,

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.580
which is an ongoing
conversation with my therapist,

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.630
but "Kotaro Lives Alone" is just, huh!

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:02.040
Oh it hurts so much,

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.803
like this poor little
boy just living alone.

00:15:07.063 --> 00:15:09.210
And as it unfolds, I
don't wanna spoil stuff,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.710
but like, whatever, if you
haven't watched it yet,

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.000
what are you doing with your life?

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:13.650
You know, he moves into this building

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:16.320
and all of these kooky,
odd, eccentric people

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:17.970
who are also living in the building

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:19.590
just like take it upon themselves

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:24.420
to become his new family,
and to take care of him,

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.610
and love him, and look out for him,

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.730
and teach him the things
that he needs to know.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.030
And it's just, it's so beautiful.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:35.670
I think like anyone
with abandonment issues

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.420
would watch that and be like,
ah, it's just so gorgeous.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.930
And I think what I really
love about anime is how,

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:46.500
for the most part, as flawed
as most characters are,

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:50.880
most of them also have like
redeeming qualities about them.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.310
So like, even though you
have these like zany,

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.920
weird characters that
are almost like losers

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:57.810
in the world of the anime,

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:01.770
their inherent goodness,
and kindness, and caring

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.690
when there is this child in their life

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:05.790
that just needs help, and support,

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:09.030
and love, and guidance is
like, it was really beautiful.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:11.670
And then on the other hand,

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.550
you have stuff like Killua and
Gon from "Hunter X Hunter."

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:15.480
- I knew you were coming here.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.359
- of course we were because I love Killua,

00:16:17.359 --> 00:16:19.230
- All right, state your case.
- so much!

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:21.780
I love Killua so much!

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.510
And he's just like such
a prickly, resentful,

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.670
standoffish little dude

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:28.050
and then going just sort of like,

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.770
blasts into his life with like his sunny,

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:36.770
almost psychotic levels of
like optimism and drive.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.240
Gon is terrifying.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.700
And that he's almost
conscienceless and that he's like

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:44.640
just so interested in
everything all the time,

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.840
regardless of whether morally
or ethically it's good or bad.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.690
And he has his very own
strict moral compass

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.070
that he follows.

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:55.110
And as he continues to
power up through the series,

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.230
I'm like, you could be
either a god or a demon.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:01.710
And killua's just like, oh Jesus, okay,

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:02.850
whatever you need bud.

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:06.330
And it's just is always there,
just like against his will,

00:17:06.330 --> 00:17:10.320
just loves his friend so much
and would do anything for him.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.540
Like to the point where he even overcame

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.730
Illumi's mind control stuff.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.140
And I think that that really is like

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.600
a true representation of
the power of friendship,

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:21.420
that like, through friendship
and through healing,

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.270
through this friendship, you
know, he's become a stronger,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.520
better version of himself,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:29.400
a more kind, softer,
empathetic version of himself.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:32.880
It's that old sort of villain
hero trope where it's like,

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.820
if you're alone then like
you can never be heard,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.330
and people don't let you
down and blah, blah blah.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:40.920
And it's like, no, actually,

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.320
through like the power
of love and friendship,

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:48.320
you can become a stronger,
better version of yourself.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.820
And that's how I feel about
"Hunter X Hunter," so.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.530
- Well-
- Do you have a rebuttal?

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:53.670
Am I wrong?

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:54.600
- I do have a rebuttal.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:55.433
Here's why you say-
- Is this gonna be

00:17:55.433 --> 00:17:57.300
the "Black Clover"
conversation all over again?

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:58.710
- I don't know them.
(Adelaide laughs)

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:03.710
Here's why Yusuke and
Kuwabara are a better example

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.030
of this ideal because-
- All right, team.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.360
- You have, no, just wait. You'll see.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.899
- I don't disagree, I'm
just being contrary,

00:18:10.899 --> 00:18:11.732
let's continue.
- You have to,

00:18:11.732 --> 00:18:15.450
see, this is our nakama
dynamic right here.

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:17.070
You have these two young men,

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:20.850
these two 14-year-old kids
who they hate one another,

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:22.410
but it's birthed out of a mutual respect

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:24.270
that neither of them understand

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.480
how to actually put into words.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:31.110
And that kind of a bond
makes both of them grow,

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:33.360
not just as individuals,
they have to grow up,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:38.360
and their rivalry that turns
into understanding and respect

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.640
not only helps them grow as fighters,

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.590
but it helps 'em grow as young men,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:46.290
learning that that kind
of toxic masculinity,

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:47.730
that rivalry that they have,

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:51.090
that's not how you have to
interact with another young man.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.660
And so there's so much
personal and paired growth

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.550
to the two of them over
the course of that.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.350
'Cause you look at those early episodes

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.210
and they're just wanting to
fight each other in the street

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:01.260
all the time.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.330
And then as you continue
moving through the series,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.780
these two have each other's
backs at every point.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:09.960
And I think there's a lot
of really great lessons

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.640
to be learned from that.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.980
And also I think that Yusuke and Kuwabara

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:17.430
could take Gon and Killua in
a fight if it came down to it.

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.860
- That's an argument we'll
have at a later date.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:20.940
- That's fine. Okay.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.185
It's gonna get too heated for

00:19:22.185 --> 00:19:23.070
the podcast.
- It'll get too heated

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.570
and then we'll waste all of this time.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.387
But I'm gonna put a pin in that

00:19:26.387 --> 00:19:28.800
- We can do that.
- to fight about later.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:32.310
Yeah, I think something
that anime does really well

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:35.100
when it's not being irritatingly horny,

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:38.070
which is a bone I have to pick
with the community in general

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:39.930
as I'm like, where are the romance animes

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:43.260
that aren't just like super
horny all the time, 'cause I'm-

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.330
- Don't bring the horny stick out on us.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:47.430
- Look, this is from
someone who's a massive fan

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:48.450
of "Food Wars."

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.100
- Oh god.
- You know?

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.110
And we know this and you
mock me for it relentlessly,

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:54.540
and that's like totally fair.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:56.910
The judges ending up naked
was always my favorite part

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:59.730
because like teenagers like that.

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:02.160
But I think what anime does really well

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:05.340
is representing close intimate,

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:07.860
communicative relationships

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:10.680
between male characters and young men,

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.690
which I think is
something severely lacking

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:13.890
in a lot of modern media.

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:18.150
And in like the raising and
education of specifically

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.430
cisgender males in general is like,

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:23.760
you're not supposed to be
emotionally close to your friends.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.990
Like women, we got that unlocked.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:28.500
That's like how we're raised
is to like be emotionally open,

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.597
and communicative, and vulnerable
with our female friends

00:20:30.597 --> 00:20:31.890
and to be affectionate with them,

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:34.500
that's totally okay, and in
fact, weird if you're like

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.780
not like that, which is a
problem in and of itself

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.340
for a different conversation.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:41.310
But I do like that in anime

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:44.970
a lot of the friendships
between men and male characters

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:46.800
and young boys, it is affectionate

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.240
and they're there for
each other emotionally,

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.277
and they communicate with each other,

00:20:50.277 --> 00:20:52.020
and when they have fights and arguments

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.250
they make up with each other.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:54.690
I think that that's really important

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.630
because I think a lot
of men don't have true

00:20:57.630 --> 00:20:59.430
emotionally intimate companionship

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:00.750
with other men in their lives.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.340
And I think that that's really problematic

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:04.290
because then their female partners

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:08.490
become like their emotional
outlet, and like frankly,

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:10.170
I ain't trying to do that kind of labor

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.960
if I'm in a relationship
with a man or a woman,

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.690
I don't wanna be their
sole emotional support.

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:17.430
It's exhausting. Nobody has it in them.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.830
So I love that that's
really, really represented

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:22.080
like through in nakama, and
then the power of friendship

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:23.490
and all of that, that's a really strong

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:24.960
recurring theme in anime.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.660
And most of like the male
anime stans that I know

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:32.070
have much healthier friendships
with their male friends

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:34.440
and have much better communication skills.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:37.410
I think as a result of growing
up with that representation

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.140
in anime, like you have
excellent communication skills,

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.678
even when you're wrong, and... (laughs)

00:21:42.678 --> 00:21:44.220
- Doesn't happen a lot, but okay, go on.

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.860
- Yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah. Okay, okay.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:50.580
But like all the male weebs
that I know, generally speaking,

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:53.310
I said generally, 'cause I
know that there are a lot of

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:57.090
like anime stans that are
shitty communicators and suck

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:58.950
as there is in all fandoms ever.

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:03.330
But like you and our Annie
hoes, like Tony and Mason,

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.430
and Jacques, are all very communicative

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:06.900
and like very emotionally in touch

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.280
with their male friends,

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:11.220
and hence with like their female
friends and their partners.

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:12.420
And that's really wonderful.

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:14.250
And I don't think it can be overstated

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:16.230
that I do think that's a direct result

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:19.920
of the representation
of friendships in anime.

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:21.030
- I think one of the best examples

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:22.530
of what you're talking about right now

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:25.290
is in "My Hero Academia."
- Mm-hm.

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:29.670
- I think Deku is such
an emotional character

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:32.880
and it's presented, not
necessarily as a flaw,

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:35.940
but as something for him to overcome.

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:38.850
The confidence is so much
about what his journey is

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:42.300
and a lot of that happens
through his interactions

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.800
with his classmates.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:47.160
Obviously his relationship
with Bakugo is very particular

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.320
because Bakugo is not a good friend.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:50.490
Look the boy's got growth,

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:54.300
- But Bakugo himself is an
extremely emotional character.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:56.053
They're two sides of the same coin,

00:22:56.053 --> 00:22:59.010
which is I think why they're
set up in opposition.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.590
Bakugo is, you know, that vaunted version

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:04.170
of male emotionality, which
is anger is the only emotion

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:06.750
you're allowed to express,
and there's no vulnerability

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:08.340
and he has to be tough,
and he has to be the best.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:13.340
And you know, his anger is not,
well almost like dismissed,

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:16.080
but is accepted and almost encouraged

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:18.480
as like a part of his masculinity

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.150
and his like hero whatever,
and it's permissible.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:25.500
It's like he has permission
to be angry and pop off,

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:27.480
which I think is problematic
for him and his character

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.490
development initially because, you know,

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:32.280
it's socially acceptable for
young men to be aggressive

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.800
especially, you know,
or training like that.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:38.310
And it's violence, you're
fighting, literally, the villains.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:41.790
And I think that his sort
of emotional development,

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:43.920
you know, when you go too far into anger,

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:47.430
it's like anger becomes hatred, whatever,

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:48.780
and hatred becomes evil.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:51.480
Like he walks a very fine line

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.610
between ultimately becoming a villain

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:56.910
if he doesn't learn to temper that anger.

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:59.760
And then by that same token,
Deku in his, you know,

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:01.590
he's deeply emotional as well.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:04.110
He cries a lot, and sure
he has his insecurity,

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:06.870
but ultimately he's like his
determination and his faith

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:09.000
in himself, and then his
friend's faith in him,

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:10.470
carries him through.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:12.720
And I think it's very cool

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:14.460
that even though he's a crybaby,

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:16.170
and even though he gets teased for it,

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:19.757
he does exist in that emotional
space, and lives in it

00:24:19.757 --> 00:24:21.180
through his friends.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:24.210
And as he gains confidence
in his training, you know,

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:28.080
all of us have to learn to
control and manage our emotions

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.690
in healthy and productive ways.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:34.110
Both of those sets of
like emotional outputs

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.530
can either be used for
good, as like motivation

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.800
to be better and to get
better and to be stronger,

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:42.990
or they could both potentially go down

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:44.310
very, very dark paths.

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:47.010
And I think that My Hero really,

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:49.160
the emotional development
of both of those kids,

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:50.792
and we have to remember
these are teenagerss.

00:24:50.792 --> 00:24:51.625
- Right.
- I don't know if you remember

00:24:51.625 --> 00:24:54.450
being a teenager, my emotions
were all over the place.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:58.050
That takes years to learn how
to not only not feel ashamed

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:03.030
of being emotional, or being
out of control emotionally,

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:06.600
but also to like learn
to breathe through it

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.790
and not let those emotional outbursts

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:09.960
hurt the people around you.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:10.925
- And I think that's one of the things

00:25:10.925 --> 00:25:14.160
that I appreciate about how
the creator, Kohei Horikoshi,

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:15.930
has done Bakugo's character.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:18.780
Because he could have very
easily taken Bakugo down

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:19.620
a dark path.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:20.453
- That would have been
- I would be very easy.

00:25:20.453 --> 00:25:21.286
- almost too predictable though.

00:25:21.286 --> 00:25:22.650
- Yes.
- He did a wonderful job

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:24.930
at like, showing that development.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:28.980
- Because that anger isn't
making him a villain,

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.810
it's just a part of who he is.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.310
It's something that he has to deal with.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:33.660
It's something that he has to overcome.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.000
And I think he's started doing that,

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:38.280
especially where we are in the manga now,

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:41.370
that just because he isn't able to control

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:43.680
or express his emotions that well,

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:45.450
it doesn't make him a bad person.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:47.640
Which is a really
important lesson to learn.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:50.160
Like you're saying for those
years, those teenage years,

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:51.610
that's something that we need to hear.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.400
- Yeah, that we all have to learn.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.890
'cause I think that that when we as people

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:58.980
get really overly emotional,
no matter what emotion it is,

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:00.450
I think particularly young men,

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:03.270
but also young women
or non-binary persons,

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:05.670
when those feelings are so
big and so overwhelming,

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.590
there's a lot of shame
that comes along with it,

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.450
because societally speaking,

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:11.100
neither men nor women are supposed to have

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:12.390
these like really big emotional,

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:15.570
like you're seen as unstable,
or unhinged, or dangerous

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:16.950
in some way.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.100
I think like seeing a
representation of that in media

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:23.070
and in anime in particular,
and then the growth

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:26.550
into like these feelings are okay to have,

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.950
it's all right to have big feelings,

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:31.470
and learning to deal with
them in a healthy way

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:35.190
instead of like repression and
then explosion, or you know,

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.410
wallowing to the point of toxicity,

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:40.350
or lashing out, either
at yourself or others,

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:41.970
is like super-valuable.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:45.000
It's super valuable to see
that growth represented.

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.523
And I think it's very
educational in like, a weird way.

00:26:47.523 --> 00:26:50.550
Like I myself had anger
issues for many, many years,

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:54.120
and I had to learn how to deal with it.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:57.270
And it's hard 'cause I thought
I was sad, and it's like, no,

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:00.540
actually I'm really like
deeply profoundly angry

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:03.030
about a bunch of stuff
that's happened in my life.

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:05.340
And part of my therapeutic
work through that was, one,

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:07.320
recognizing that I was angry.

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.420
And then two, recognizing that anger,

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:11.850
much like joy, is a
very energetic emotion.

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:14.640
And if you try and push it
down, it has to come out,

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.350
it's energy, it has to come out.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:17.880
And it can come out in either destructive

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:19.680
or productive ways, and
I think that's the lesson

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:20.820
that like Bakugo was learning.

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:25.650
He can channel that anger
into productive pursuits

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:28.050
once given the tools to like communicate

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.000
and understand where it's coming from

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.380
and what he could do with it,

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.430
both good and bad.

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:33.540
You can channel that.

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:35.790
So like once I figured that out,

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:39.930
channeled that energy into like
cleaning my house, you know,

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:42.270
or going to the gym or like, whatever,

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:43.500
and it's really useful.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.270
Anger is a very useful emotion.

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:46.620
All emotions are very useful

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.330
if you utilize them appropriately.

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:50.400
- And kind of bringing it back to theme,

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:55.400
a lot of the ways that
both Deku and Bakugo learn

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.890
how to do those things is
through their relationships

00:27:58.890 --> 00:28:00.677
with other characters
- Through opposition, mm-hm.

00:28:00.677 --> 00:28:02.970
- Making friendships,
not just with each other,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:04.980
but when you start to, what's
interesting is, you know,

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.170
you have those two kind of
factions that sort of form

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:08.912
around both of those characters.

00:28:08.912 --> 00:28:11.348
Bakugo has like Kirishima and the Eijiro-

00:28:11.348 --> 00:28:12.784
- He has the Bakusquad, and then there's

00:28:12.784 --> 00:28:13.617
the Dekusquad.
- He's got his Bakusquad.

00:28:13.617 --> 00:28:15.720
- Yep.
- And so in having people

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:19.080
see past what might be
surface level for you,

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:22.200
or what you project out
there and really see, okay,

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:25.350
you might be a crybaby or you
might be an angry asshole,

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.780
there's a lot of other stuff
that I can see behind you

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:29.160
and now I'm gonna ride with you.

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:33.780
And that bolstering, that
idea of people seeing you,

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:37.200
and accepting you, flaws
and all, is really powerful.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:39.180
- Not only for like
the 10% that they know,

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:40.800
but for the 90% they don't know.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.030
- Yep.
- For like the potential

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:44.520
they see in addition to the flaws

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:45.720
that are already present.

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:47.070
I mean, I feel that way about my friends,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.200
like I've seen my friends
through like good times

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.360
and bad times, and when
they're their best selves,

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:52.680
and when they have not
been their best selves,

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:54.240
and they've seen me the same way

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.833
and they still love me, warts and all.

00:28:56.833 --> 00:29:01.320
From rooftop to basement,
the good and the bad,

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:03.450
the really beautiful and the really ugly.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:07.470
They love me 100% for everything I am

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:10.467
and for everything I will be
and everything I have been.

00:29:10.467 --> 00:29:15.180
And I think that that's ultimately
what friendship is about.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:17.880
- So along those lines, if you were to,

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.010
if you were to pick like your nakama,

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:24.210
like you're building your
crew, your pro-hero agency,

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:27.330
who are some characters
from anime that you feel

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.090
you would want by your
side for whatever reason?

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:32.010
Just because you love them,

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:34.050
because they've got skill
sets that you don't,

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.040
because that's also what it's about.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:37.320
You know, friendships and
team building and stuff.

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.110
Everyone in a group of
friends isn't always the same.

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:42.180
You're filling gaps.
- Well, I would hope not,

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:43.170
exactly.
- Yeah.

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:44.463
- Yeah, and you want people around you

00:29:44.463 --> 00:29:49.410
that like inspire you to
be different or you know,

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:51.600
that have cool stuff going on.

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:53.040
That's how I pick my friends anyway.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.630
Like, people I think are really inspiring,

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:56.220
and really impressive,
and who I look up to

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.560
for whatever personality traits they have

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.890
that I feel I'm lacking, you know?

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:04.650
Okay, can I pick from like any anime ever?

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:05.820
- Yeah. Go for it.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:09.093
- Hmm, I mean,

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:13.950
okay, so from "One Piece,"
just the whole crew.

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:15.270
I'd like to be part of my hero team.

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:16.915
From "Hunter X Hunter"

00:30:16.915 --> 00:30:19.153
I'd say, Killua and Gon I really love.

00:30:19.153 --> 00:30:20.370
I really love Bisky.

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:22.440
I know a lot of people
don't really love Bisky,

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.490
but I really love Bisky,

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:25.320
and we can talk about jewelry
and gemstones and stuff.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:27.240
So I really love Bisky
from "Hunter X Hunter"

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:29.160
and I don't wanna hear anything about it.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.380
I want Faye from "Cowboy Bebop."

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.050
And also Ein.
- Also Ein, nope, fair.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:34.883
You need an animal.

00:30:34.883 --> 00:30:36.322
- Yeah, I love Ein.

00:30:36.322 --> 00:30:39.057
(Adelaide hums)

00:30:39.057 --> 00:30:41.070
I'd want Miss Macrophage
from "Souls at Work."

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:42.930
She is a baller.

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:43.763
- No, that's a good one.

00:30:43.763 --> 00:30:46.800
- Miss Macrophage just like
lays the hurt all the time,

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:47.633
and then when she isn't,

00:30:47.633 --> 00:30:50.550
she's like la la la la la la
la la la, with all the babies.

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:53.400
Let's see, I'd want Hawk
from "Seven Deadly Sins"

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:54.690
just 'cause I like pigs.

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:56.550
I'd want "Sailor Moon"
to be part of my squad

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.310
as a literal goddess with
the ability to tear apart

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:00.330
the fabric of the universe.

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:02.130
I just wanna like keep my eye on her,

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:02.963
just want her to be around.

00:31:02.963 --> 00:31:04.140
- So you bring up "Sailor Moon,"

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:06.390
which on its own, phenomenal series,

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:08.610
and one of the really
cool things about it is

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:12.480
it's such a good representation
of female friendship.

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:13.890
- Yeah, it really is.

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:17.100
I mean, like every member of that group

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.260
has their own like distinct
individual personalities

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:20.610
and they all get on or don't get on,

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:22.677
or have their like issues with each other

00:31:22.677 --> 00:31:24.390
on a regular basis.

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:26.190
Like much with true friendships,

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:28.290
like there's sometimes when
you're just not gonna vibe

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:29.790
or you have arguments or you have fights,

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:31.980
and they're always so
communicative and fantastic

00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:33.300
about coming back together.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:34.650
And then also about like,

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:36.870
keeping the main goal and focus,

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:39.510
no matter what kind of
arguments or internal struggles

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:41.760
they're having, they all
show up for each other

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:42.990
when push comes to shove,

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:45.660
like when it's really
crunch time, and it's like,

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:47.100
I'm gotta save the universe again.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.040
They all show up and they put it aside.

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:52.110
And I think through a lot
of those battles, you know,

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.839
they come out the other end
just being like, that's right,

00:31:54.839 --> 00:31:59.190
the true friendship exists
beyond these petty disputes,

00:31:59.190 --> 00:32:00.630
which I think is a really
good representation

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:01.860
of true friendship.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:06.360
I mean, sure we're not like,
saving the world in real life,

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:07.890
but you know, it is a good reminder

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:10.680
that at the end of the day,
true friendship exists,

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.950
like beyond and outside all
of these like petty arguments,

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:15.600
or whatever that you might have.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:19.500
I really like the friendship
in "Kakegurui" as well,

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:23.730
that sort of develops
between our lead girl,

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:25.683
and all the names are escaping me,

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:29.850
our little blonde pigtail bestie

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:33.780
who is such a bitch when
you first start the series,

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:36.930
and just watching their
friendship kind of grow.

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:40.650
And it's just a hilarious case
of like the black-haired girl

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:42.270
has the golden retriever energy,

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:45.570
and the blonde is like
the mean black cat energy.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:48.810
And just watching those two go from being

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.270
kind of contentious
and like oil and water,

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:52.710
to such fast friends

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:55.460
that they're just like wrecking
people's shit together.

00:32:56.376 --> 00:32:59.474
They got there through
like mutual respect,

00:32:59.474 --> 00:33:02.580
and cooperation, and really
play on each other's like

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:06.660
strengths and knowledge to
ultimately succeed together.

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:08.940
And as they bring like other
women into their group,

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:11.340
and I think the representation
of female friendships

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:13.740
in anime, especially how
they grow and develop

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:16.410
is also really important in a world

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:18.780
where a lot of women are sort
of pitted against each other

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:19.620
for whatever reason.

00:33:19.620 --> 00:33:22.650
Like seeing representation
of women working together

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:26.172
is really important and fabulous.

00:33:26.172 --> 00:33:28.017
And I love "Kakegurui" as well.

00:33:28.017 --> 00:33:29.580
- See we're using all these great examples

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:30.600
and I think it should be noted,

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:32.070
for those of you who don't know,

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:34.650
the creator of "Sailor Moon"

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:37.590
and of the creator of "Yu Yu
Hakusho" and "Hunter X Hunter"

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:40.320
are married in real life.
- They're married.

00:33:40.320 --> 00:33:41.342
- That is-

00:33:41.342 --> 00:33:44.054
- It's the coolest.
- anime and manga royalty

00:33:44.054 --> 00:33:46.560
right there.
- That's the king and queen.

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:51.360
That's like the royal couple
of anime, which is so cool.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:55.770
And they both just like clearly
know exactly what nakama

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:57.690
power of Friendship is all about.

